1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,192
The school outline names normal delivery and several complications. We
will follow the clinical sequence from recognizing impending birth
through separate maternal and newborn reassessment.

2
00:00:12,192 --> 00:00:24,432
The narrative foundation is the reviewed birth and postpartum sections
of the supplied Nancy textbook. Current local procedures qualify older
instructions rather than being silently mixed with them.

3
00:00:24,432 --> 00:00:33,336
This is preparation for supervised practical learning, not an added
school online module or a claim that reading certifies delivery
maneuvers.

4
00:00:33,336 --> 00:00:44,568
The newborn resuscitation algorithm is a separate next session. Here,
always prepare for it while maintaining maternal care and recognizing
when birth has become complicated.

5
00:00:44,568 --> 00:00:53,832
The first stage ends when the cervix is fully dilated. Effacement
describes thinning and shortening, while dilation describes opening.

6
00:00:53,832 --> 00:01:02,832
The second stage extends from complete dilation until the baby is
born. Do not define its start simply as the head entering the birth
canal.

7
00:01:02,832 --> 00:01:13,536
The third stage ends when the placenta is delivered. Maternal bleeding
and physiology require observation throughout, not only after the
placenta appears.

8
00:01:13,536 --> 00:01:26,904
Textbook average times provide context but do not predict an
individual birth or justify waiting through deterioration. A previous
rapid delivery and the current signs can matter more than an average
clock.

9
00:01:26,904 --> 00:01:37,512
Measure contraction frequency from the start of one contraction to the
start of the next, and describe duration and intensity separately. Ask
whether the pattern is changing.

10
00:01:37,512 --> 00:01:48,696
A mucus discharge with some blood may be show. Heavy bleeding, shock
or severe persistent pain should not be relabelled normal labour just
because contractions are present.

11
00:01:48,696 --> 00:02:01,872
Record whether membranes have ruptured, when, and whether fluid was
clear, bloody or meconium-stained. Fluid appearance is a clue, not a
precise clock or definitive fetal diagnosis.

12
00:02:01,872 --> 00:02:13,080
Do not rupture membranes or perform an internal examination to
accelerate assessment. Continue the maternal primary survey and ask
the questions that affect immediate preparation.

13
00:02:13,080 --> 00:02:24,120
Ask about an uncontrollable urge to bear down, grunting or a feeling
of needing a bowel movement. These may signal descent and a rapidly
approaching birth.

14
00:02:24,120 --> 00:02:35,856
Explain and obtain appropriate permission for necessary external
observation with privacy. A presenting part visible between
contractions is a stronger sign than contraction timing alone.

15
00:02:35,856 --> 00:02:46,872
Prepare without trying to hold the baby back or insist on walking to a
vehicle. The safest location depends on imminent delivery, resources
and the immediate environment.

16
00:02:46,872 --> 00:02:57,048
Do not confuse a leg, arm, cord or buttocks with a normal head-first
presentation. Recognizing what is actually visible changes the
pathway.

17
00:02:57,048 --> 00:03:07,800
After birth, the placenta separates and the uterus contracts to help
compress vessels at the attachment site. Failure of these mechanisms
can produce major bleeding.

18
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:16,608
At the same time, the newborn must establish breathing and maintain
warmth. Allocate care so both patients remain observed.

19
00:03:16,608 --> 00:03:25,992
Do not pull the cord to speed the placenta or assume a long third
stage is safe while bleeding continues. The patient's condition
determines urgency.

20
00:03:25,992 --> 00:03:36,864
Arrange indicated conveyance without waiting simply to finish the
stage on scene. Communicate whether the placenta has delivered and
what bleeding and perfusion have done.

21
00:03:36,864 --> 00:03:48,384
Ask about previous births and whether any were rapid. Previous
experience can help the patient recognize that birth is close, but it
does not eliminate the need for assessment.

22
00:03:48,384 --> 00:03:59,400
Use current signs and relevant external observation, not a single
contraction interval as proof of inevitable timing. Do not attempt
internal dilation measurement.

23
00:03:59,400 --> 00:04:10,728
Consider the whole move: carrying, loading, road conditions and the
ability to deliver safely en route. A long extraction may make
immediate preparation necessary.

24
00:04:10,728 --> 00:04:21,552
Request help early and explain the plan. A prepared scene can still
change rapidly if malpresentation, maternal instability or newborn
compromise develops.

25
00:04:21,552 --> 00:04:32,808
When birth is not imminent, arrange appropriate conveyance while
monitoring progression and maternal status. Avoid promises that birth
cannot occur before arrival.

26
00:04:32,808 --> 00:04:42,648
When birth is imminent, prepare a safe supported delivery and newborn
area. Do not create a dangerous transfer merely to avoid attending a
birth.

27
00:04:42,648 --> 00:04:55,848
A complication can require urgent specialized care while immediate
permitted maneuvers occur. Follow the applicable branch and coordinate
resources rather than use one transport rule for every presentation.

28
00:04:55,848 --> 00:05:07,536
If birth becomes imminent during conveyance, coordinate a safe stop
when feasible. Effective care and safe vehicle operation matter more
than continuing movement at all costs.

29
00:05:07,536 --> 00:05:18,864
Allocate responsibilities before birth and request additional trained
help when needed. A two-person crew may face competing tasks that
cannot all be performed safely at once.

30
00:05:18,864 --> 00:05:30,000
One provider must continue maternal observation while newborn support
begins. A loud cry attracts attention, but it does not show that the
mother is perfusing well.

31
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:40,824
Assign communication and timing, including birth time, cord actions
and changes in either patient. Use closed-loop requests for equipment
and escalation.

32
00:05:40,824 --> 00:05:53,520
The diagram is a team-planning aid, not a replacement delivery
algorithm. If one patient deteriorates, explicitly redistribute roles
instead of silently leaving the other unobserved.

33
00:05:53,520 --> 00:06:05,952
Use appropriate infection precautions and splash protection. Open and
handle the actual birth kit according to its packaging and training
rather than assume every kit contains the same supplies.

34
00:06:05,952 --> 00:06:18,744
Prepare dry towels, covering, cord equipment and pads while preserving
as much cleanliness and privacy as feasible. A controlled birth takes
priority over perfect draping when time is short.

35
00:06:18,744 --> 00:06:32,184
Check suitable newborn mask and ventilation equipment, suction, warmth
and assessment tools before they are needed. Merely finding a bag does
not prove its valves, mask fit or oxygen configuration work.

36
00:06:32,184 --> 00:06:43,128
Identify missing skills or equipment and request help early. Do not
perform an invasive intervention outside role simply because a device
happens to be present.

37
00:06:43,128 --> 00:06:55,368
Explain the proposed position and ask the patient what helps. A
supported lateral, semi-reclined or other appropriate position may be
useful according to the situation and trained approach.

38
00:06:55,368 --> 00:07:05,160
Use a stable surface and adequate limb support. Do not force hip
movement through pain or choose a position only because it is
convenient for the assistant.

39
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:15,912
Maintain access for controlled support and newborn care while avoiding
unnecessary exposure. Reassess breathing and perfusion after a major
position change.

40
00:07:15,912 --> 00:07:26,976
Alternative positions are not guarantees against tearing or
aspiration. Water-birth physiology claims in the old text are not
taught here as permission or a safety assurance.

41
00:07:26,976 --> 00:07:36,384
Use the trained controlled-support technique as the head emerges.
Communicate calmly with the patient rather than shout competing
pushing instructions.

42
00:07:36,384 --> 00:07:46,992
Support the head and allow its normal turning. The task is not an
extraction: do not forcibly pull, twist the neck or hold the
presenting part back.

43
00:07:46,992 --> 00:07:58,368
If membranes cover an already delivered face, clear them appropriately
through the trained birth approach. Do not confuse this with
deliberately rupturing membranes before delivery.

44
00:07:58,368 --> 00:08:08,400
A slide cannot show the safe amount or direction of hand support.
Demonstrate the maneuver on a simulator with the instructor before
claiming practical competence.

45
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:17,808
Check for a nuchal cord using the trained gentle method after the head
is delivered. A loose loop may be reduced without pulling hard on the
cord.

46
00:08:17,808 --> 00:08:32,280
If the cord is tight and cannot be reduced, use the current
complication pathway and seek help. Local pages differ in how they
describe the cutting condition, so do not turn every loop into an
automatic clamp-and-cut step.

47
00:08:32,280 --> 00:08:45,024
If clamping and cutting is required under the applicable trained
pathway, birth must be completed promptly and newborn support must be
ready. Do not cut casually while the body remains trapped.

48
00:08:45,024 --> 00:08:54,504
Preserve maternal and newborn observation throughout. Recognition of
another complication, such as shoulder dystocia, changes the urgent
plan.

49
00:08:54,504 --> 00:09:05,880
As the shoulders deliver, use the trained gentle guidance and support.
Do not interpret the textbook directional descriptions as permission
to pull strongly on the neck.

50
00:09:05,880 --> 00:09:15,648
Be ready to support the slippery body as it follows. Maintain secure
support without compressing the chest or leaving the newborn suspended
by its head.

51
00:09:15,648 --> 00:09:26,736
If the shoulders do not follow normally, stop forceful attempts,
announce the emergency and use the shoulder-dystocia response. More
traction is not a solution.

52
00:09:26,736 --> 00:09:34,368
Note the birth time when the baby is completely delivered and begin
immediate newborn assessment while maternal care continues.

53
00:09:34,368 --> 00:09:46,368
Assess tone, breathing and heart rate promptly. An apneic or gasping
newborn needs the appropriate immediate pathway, not a wait until a
scheduled score is calculated.

54
00:09:46,368 --> 00:09:57,360
For a well newborn, provide appropriate observed skin-to-skin care and
thermal protection. Keep the face visible and the airway supported
while continuing evaluation.

55
00:09:57,360 --> 00:10:08,448
Routine suction is not needed simply because delivery occurred or
fluid is meconium-stained. Actual obstruction and the current newborn
guideline determine airway clearing.

56
00:10:08,448 --> 00:10:19,824
The next lecture teaches effective ventilation and the school first-
minute drill in detail. Meanwhile, do not let newborn care leave the
mother without bleeding and perfusion assessment.

57
00:10:19,824 --> 00:10:32,352
Healthy vigorous newborns generally receive deferred cord clamping
while assessment and warmth continue. Do not require a compulsory
position at the vaginal level to make skin-to-skin care possible.

58
00:10:32,352 --> 00:10:45,744
Current local pages are not identical: L zero two describes at least
two minutes for vigorous infants, while M zero nine says at least
sixty seconds. Keep the page and situation explicit.

59
00:10:45,744 --> 00:10:58,992
Agree on the current operational plan with the trained team and
instructor. Do not postpone necessary effective ventilation merely to
satisfy a cord timer or import unreviewed cord-milking techniques.

60
00:10:58,992 --> 00:11:11,232
When cutting is performed, use the actual trained clamp placement, cut
between secure clamps and check for bleeding. Continue assessment
rather than assume a completed cut ends cord risk.

61
00:11:11,232 --> 00:11:22,776
Continue maternal observation as the placenta separates. Timing
varies, and ongoing bleeding or instability is more important than
waiting for the end of a quoted normal interval.

62
00:11:22,776 --> 00:11:33,696
Do not pull on the cord, reach in for retained tissue or attempt
active placental delivery outside role. These actions can cause
serious harm including inversion.

63
00:11:33,696 --> 00:11:43,560
If the placenta delivers, preserve it appropriately for hospital
assessment and describe obvious findings without claiming complete
exclusion of retained tissue.

64
00:11:43,560 --> 00:11:53,736
Do not delay indicated conveyance solely to wait for delivery of the
placenta. Hand over whether it has delivered, the bleeding trend and
maternal response.

65
00:11:53,736 --> 00:12:05,256
Repeat the maternal primary assessment, visible bleeding and relevant
symptoms. Do not let the attention given to a newborn conceal ongoing
hemorrhage or neurological illness.

66
00:12:05,256 --> 00:12:15,792
Continue newborn observation even when the first cry is strong.
Temperature, breathing and response can change during wrapping,
transfer or transport.

67
00:12:15,792 --> 00:12:26,184
Support bonding and appropriate feeding when both are clinically
suitable, but never use breastfeeding as a substitute for required
resuscitation or hemorrhage control.

68
00:12:26,184 --> 00:12:35,472
Keep separate records and handover information for each patient. A
shared scene does not mean one set of observations can stand for both.

69
00:12:35,472 --> 00:12:45,432
Name what you actually see and when it occurred. A head already
delivered with shoulders failing to follow is different from a
presenting limb before the body is born.

70
00:12:45,432 --> 00:12:57,048
Declare the complication clearly, ask for the appropriate help and
assign continued maternal and newborn responsibilities. Do not let a
mnemonic hide an uncertain presentation.

71
00:12:57,048 --> 00:13:08,856
Use only the permitted trained steps for that emergency while
coordinating time-sensitive conveyance. Some specialist maneuvers
listed in textbooks are not authorized for the ambulance role.

72
00:13:08,856 --> 00:13:18,480
Continue reassessment and note the response to each step. Neither a
fixed time limit nor a named maneuver guarantees successful delivery.

73
00:13:18,480 --> 00:13:29,424
The school emphasizes shoulder dystocia when the shoulder becomes
impacted and birth fails to progress after the head delivers.
Retraction of the head can be a clue.

74
00:13:29,424 --> 00:13:40,896
Call for help immediately and track time. Previous dystocia or a
larger fetus may increase concern, but absence of those clues does not
exclude the emergency.

75
00:13:40,896 --> 00:13:51,792
Prepare for newborn compromise after birth while continuing maternal
support. The immediate objective is a coordinated permitted response,
not progressively stronger pulling.

76
00:13:51,792 --> 00:14:01,080
Do not press on the uterine fundus or apply forceful head and neck
traction. Those are not substitutes for the supervised maneuvers
taught next.

77
00:14:01,080 --> 00:14:11,472
Explain the emergency briefly and coordinate assistance. McRoberts
changes the maternal pelvic relationship through hip flexion; it does
not pull the baby out.

78
00:14:11,472 --> 00:14:21,600
Support the legs and bring the thighs toward the abdomen using the
trained method. Respect injury, pain and the practical support needed
on the actual surface.

79
00:14:21,600 --> 00:14:32,784
Use the maneuver with the appropriate coordinated birth response and
reassess progression. Avoid twisting unsupported limbs or assuming
more force is always more effective.

80
00:14:32,784 --> 00:14:43,656
Practise on a simulator with an instructor who can assess hand
position, support and coordination. Verbal recall alone cannot
establish a safe practical performance.

81
00:14:43,656 --> 00:14:55,536
The school pairs McRoberts with suprapubic pressure to help release an
impacted shoulder. Suprapubic identifies the area above the pubic
bone, not the upper uterine fundus.

82
00:14:55,536 --> 00:15:07,656
Direction, hand placement and timing must follow the actual supervised
technique. This lecture distinguishes the concept but does not claim a
safe direction can be learned from a label alone.

83
00:15:07,656 --> 00:15:15,456
Do not substitute downward fundal pressure. Do not increase head or
neck traction because the first attempt did not progress.

84
00:15:15,456 --> 00:15:26,088
Reassess the result and coordinate the next permitted step or
escalation. Make the response visible to the team rather than repeat
an ineffective maneuver indefinitely.

85
00:15:26,088 --> 00:15:36,024
L zero eight explicitly labels several HELPERR steps not authorized.
Knowing the whole mnemonic is not permission to perform every action
in it.

86
00:15:36,024 --> 00:15:49,032
The prohibited entries include episiotomy, internal rotational
maneuvers and removal of the posterior arm. Do not import them from a
midwifery or physician description into PCP practice.

87
00:15:49,032 --> 00:15:59,760
Hands-and-knees repositioning may be part of the local trained
response when feasible. It still needs support, communication and
attention to the actual maternal situation.

88
00:15:59,760 --> 00:16:12,336
Local escalation timing is an upper planning boundary, not an
instruction to wait until it expires. Seek help early, assess response
and coordinate urgent movement without unsafe delay.

89
00:16:12,336 --> 00:16:25,176
Breech presentation means buttocks or feet lead rather than the head.
Hospital and expert support are preferred when feasible, but the
actual stage may make birth on scene unavoidable.

90
00:16:25,176 --> 00:16:36,576
If birth is already progressing, follow the trained local support
sequence and do not pull on the body or neck. Avoid converting an
ankle-lift illustration into uncontrolled traction.

91
00:16:36,576 --> 00:16:45,672
A delayed head is an immediate emergency requiring resources and
urgent planning, not a reason to wait for a quoted threshold before
acting.

92
00:16:45,672 --> 00:16:57,264
Textbook internal-finger and named obstetric rescue techniques are not
independent PCP permissions. Protect both patients and coordinate the
appropriate assistance and conveyance.

93
00:16:57,264 --> 00:17:09,720
A visible single limb can indicate a serious abnormal presentation.
Describe exactly what is seen and avoid assuming it is an
uncomplicated footling breech without the broader context.

94
00:17:09,720 --> 00:17:19,104
Support the mother, protect exposed tissue and keep warm through the
appropriate pathway. Do not manipulate the limb to test whether it
will move.

95
00:17:19,104 --> 00:17:28,392
Do not push the part back or attempt extraction. Arrange urgent
conveyance and notification to a facility able to manage the
complication.

96
00:17:28,392 --> 00:17:39,912
Continue maternal primary assessment and communicate the presentation
and timing. A rare presentation still requires reliable basic care
while specialist help is arranged.

97
00:17:39,912 --> 00:17:50,664
A cord ahead of the baby may become compressed against the presenting
part and pelvis. Declare the emergency and organize immediate
appropriate help and conveyance.

98
00:17:50,664 --> 00:17:59,328
Minimize handling and protect the exposed cord according to the
current pathway. Do not push it back, repeatedly palpate it or pull on
it.

99
00:17:59,328 --> 00:18:12,624
Use the permitted positioning and oxygen instructions while
maintaining maternal care. A stationary knee-chest position is not a
safe restrained transport position; the movement plan must change
appropriately.

100
00:18:12,624 --> 00:18:25,944
Current L zero eight marks internal elevation of the fetal head not
authorized, despite its description in the textbook. Do not adopt that
invasive maneuver without actual authorization and training.

101
00:18:25,944 --> 00:18:37,752
Meconium-stained fluid should be documented and increases concern for
respiratory difficulty. It does not tell you an exact time of fetal
compromise or prove aspiration has occurred.

102
00:18:37,752 --> 00:18:48,336
Assess breathing, tone and heart rate immediately. Prepare appropriate
airway support rather than repeatedly suction a newborn who needs
effective ventilation.

103
00:18:48,336 --> 00:19:02,376
The supplied text includes old aggressive and tracheal-suction
descriptions. Routine suction simply for meconium, including routine
tracheal suction in a nonvigorous infant, is not the current teaching
sequence.

104
00:19:02,376 --> 00:19:14,328
Clear genuine obstruction with the appropriate authorized method and
reassess effect. The newborn session will distinguish the remaining
local-page wording from current obstruction-led guidance.

105
00:19:14,328 --> 00:19:26,280
Preterm describes gestation below thirty-seven weeks; low birth weight
describes measured mass. They often overlap but neither can be
inferred precisely from appearance alone.

106
00:19:26,280 --> 00:19:38,136
Request appropriate neonatal help and prepare thermal and respiratory
support. Do not apply an old oxygen-tent recipe or blanket drying
instruction to every gestational age.

107
00:19:38,136 --> 00:19:53,424
Multiple gestation means additional patients, equipment and possible
complications. Label each newborn, record separate birth times and
continue separate assessment; smaller size does not guarantee an
easier breech birth.

108
00:19:53,424 --> 00:20:03,600
Do not let care of a second infant leave the mother unobserved.
Recheck bleeding, uterine context and perfusion while resources are
redistributed.

109
00:20:03,600 --> 00:20:12,528
Uterine contraction normally compresses vessels where the placenta
separated. Atony leaves that mechanical bleeding control inadequate.

110
00:20:12,528 --> 00:20:20,664
Birth-related tears can bleed even when the fundus is firm. Do not
assume every postpartum hemorrhage has a soft uterus.

111
00:20:20,664 --> 00:20:32,328
Retained tissue can interfere with effective contraction, while
clotting dysfunction can worsen bleeding. The Four Ts are a search
framework, not proof of the exact cause in the field.

112
00:20:32,328 --> 00:20:42,792
Consider more than one cause at once and treat current physiology.
Recognizing a plausible cause must not delay hemorrhage support and
definitive care.

113
00:20:42,792 --> 00:20:54,648
The traditional five-hundred-milliliter definition is a reference, not
a safe allowance. Ongoing blood loss with hypovolemia requires urgent
concern even before that amount is measured.

114
00:20:54,648 --> 00:21:06,000
Observe and collect loss when feasible and record the trend. Do not
invent exact milliliters from a quick look or ignore blood retained in
material or concealed internally.

115
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:16,752
Repeat perfusion, responsiveness and reliable vital signs. A normal
initial pressure or a firm fundus does not independently exclude
serious hemorrhage.

116
00:21:16,752 --> 00:21:26,664
Declare the concern, mobilize resources and communicate early. Do not
finish a perfect paperwork estimate before starting indicated care.

117
00:21:26,664 --> 00:21:39,072
Assess fundal tone and record whether the placenta has delivered. A
firm uterus and an atonic uterus suggest different searches, while
neither finding completes the whole assessment.

118
00:21:39,072 --> 00:21:48,696
Routine massage before placental delivery should not be used to force
separation. L zero two describes massage after delivery of the
placenta.

119
00:21:48,696 --> 00:22:02,880
L zero nine separately allows considering massage when actual
postpartum hemorrhage occurs before placental delivery. This is a
condition-specific exception, not a reason to massage every
undelivered placenta.

120
00:22:02,880 --> 00:22:13,176
Use the actual trained technique and consultation plan. Do not add
cord traction, internal tissue removal or a replacement attempt for
suspected inversion.

121
00:22:13,176 --> 00:22:25,416
Provide indicated airway and oxygen support, maintain warmth and
reassess perfusion. The newborn may need a separate provider while
maternal resuscitation becomes the immediate priority.

122
00:22:25,416 --> 00:22:37,848
Manage accessible external laceration bleeding with the appropriate
trained direct-pressure method. Do not perform an internal examination
or packing simply because a general text mentions it.

123
00:22:37,848 --> 00:22:52,296
Use actual current fluid and tranexamic-acid conditions, with required
prior consultation and response checks. Do not copy wide-open fluid or
an old oxytocin infusion recipe into PCP practice.

124
00:22:52,296 --> 00:23:06,504
Arrange time-sensitive definitive care and update the receiving team.
Breastfeeding or voiding when suitable are not substitutes for urgent
treatment, and an unstable patient should not be made to walk to a
toilet.

125
00:23:06,504 --> 00:23:22,320
A suspected inverted uterus may present with abnormal protruding
tissue, pain, bleeding and shock. Not every form is externally
visible, so do not require a dramatic mass before taking deterioration
seriously.

126
00:23:22,320 --> 00:23:34,008
Do not pull on an attached placenta or attempt replacement merely
because the supplied textbook describes a maneuver. A specialist
description is not independent PCP authorization.

127
00:23:34,008 --> 00:23:45,816
Protect exposed tissue appropriately and support airway, warmth and
perfusion while arranging urgent expert care. Avoid further
manipulation that could worsen bleeding or injury.

128
00:23:45,816 --> 00:23:58,176
Communicate the observed tissue, placental attachment if known,
bleeding and response. Use suspected language when the field finding
cannot establish the diagnosis.

129
00:23:58,176 --> 00:24:11,424
The school names the Midwives Regulation. For current context, the
Nurses and Midwives Regulation under the HPOA came into effect on
April first, twenty twenty-six; the current selected Part five was
checked.

130
00:24:11,424 --> 00:24:21,816
It describes midwifery assessment, care and emergency measures for
midwifery patients and their newborns and infants, subject to limits
and conditions.

131
00:24:21,816 --> 00:24:32,232
A deviation from the normal course requires consultation with a
medical practitioner or nurse practitioner and transfer of
responsibility when necessary or appropriate.

132
00:24:32,232 --> 00:24:42,576
This current-law bridge is not a complete legal opinion or a
substitute for the school exam version. Do not present a twenty
twenty-one resource sheet as the entire current law.

133
00:24:42,576 --> 00:24:54,480
BCCNM describes four controls: legislation, college requirements,
place-of-practice policies and individual competence. All must fit the
actual task and setting.

134
00:24:54,480 --> 00:25:06,696
A midwife may have newborn airway or medicine permissions that differ
from a paramedic's. That is not proof that every midwife performs
every procedure or that one profession is globally senior.

135
00:25:06,696 --> 00:25:15,408
Ask what care is underway, who is credentialed and what support is
needed. Do not infer individual competence from the job title alone.

136
00:25:15,408 --> 00:25:26,304
Their authority does not transfer to you by being present. You remain
responsible for actions within your own scope, service requirements
and appropriate consultation.

137
00:25:26,304 --> 00:25:37,536
At a midwife-attended birth, obtain a concise handover and identify
who is caring for each patient. Share the immediate concerns before
rearranging ongoing care.

138
00:25:37,536 --> 00:25:51,576
Current L zero one describes midwives as possible primary maternity
providers while maternal resuscitation remains a paramedic
responsibility. Agree the actual newborn lead according to competence
and resources.

139
00:25:51,576 --> 00:26:05,688
Communicate medicine names, doses and times already given without
assuming your role can repeat every agent. Use closed-loop requests
and appropriate consultation when responsibilities or destination are
unclear.

140
00:26:05,688 --> 00:26:19,056
Keep the patient's consent central. Midwife attendance does not
automatically clear nonconveyance; any refusal or choice requires the
actual assessment, information and applicable process.

141
00:26:19,056 --> 00:26:30,768
This original rehearsal uses an estimated thirty-eight weeks and three
days, one previous birth and an increasing urge to push. It is not the
school Wednesday scenario.

142
00:26:30,768 --> 00:26:42,480
Contractions are reported around three minutes apart, but the head is
visible between contractions. The external finding outweighs
reassurance from an interval that is not less than two minutes.

143
00:26:42,480 --> 00:26:54,048
Perform the primary assessment, explain preparation and assign
maternal and newborn tasks. Do not force the patient to walk or
perform an internal exam to prove dilation.

144
00:26:54,048 --> 00:27:03,768
Name the equipment and missing resources before birth. The objective
is a calm coordinated sequence with explicit changes if a complication
appears.

145
00:27:03,768 --> 00:27:15,240
Assume birth completes with gentle trained support and the newborn has
good tone and an effective cry. Record the complete-birth time and
start separate newborn observation.

146
00:27:15,240 --> 00:27:26,976
Use appropriate observed thermal and skin-to-skin care while following
the agreed cord plan. Do not perform routine suction or give every
vigorous baby one hundred percent oxygen.

147
00:27:26,976 --> 00:27:37,344
The mother is alert but the placenta has not delivered. Continue
bleeding and perfusion assessment rather than have the entire team
attend only to the newborn.

148
00:27:37,344 --> 00:27:46,656
Explain the next steps and prepare appropriate conveyance. A
successful birth does not finish the third-stage or newborn-transition
risks.

149
00:27:46,656 --> 00:27:59,472
Now assume the placenta has delivered, bleeding continues briskly and
the mother becomes pale and lightheaded. Pulse is one hundred twenty-
six and pressure eighty-eight over fifty-four.

150
00:27:59,472 --> 00:28:10,272
Total blood loss is uncertain and the fundus appears poorly
contracted. Do not delay recognition of hemorrhage while trying to
prove exactly five hundred milliliters.

151
00:28:10,272 --> 00:28:23,280
Redistribute tasks so maternal authorized hemorrhage care and newborn
observation both continue. Use trained fundal care and actual current
fluid and medicine conditions with required consultation.

152
00:28:23,280 --> 00:28:33,360
Pre-alert, reassess response and obtain urgent definitive care. A
crying baby or delivered placenta must not hide maternal shock.

153
00:28:33,360 --> 00:28:43,848
Provide the shared obstetric timeline with reliable sources and
uncertainties. Include gestation, presentation, complete birth and
placental status.

154
00:28:43,848 --> 00:28:55,728
Then state maternal findings and trend, observed or estimated blood
loss, tone, interventions and response. Do not replace the illness
story with normal delivery.

155
00:28:55,728 --> 00:29:06,936
Give the newborn's own breathing, heart rate, temperature,
resuscitation and timed Apgar information. A score during support
needs the support recorded beside it.

156
00:29:06,936 --> 00:29:17,544
Identify clinicians and care already provided, including medicines and
times, and remaining needs. Clear transfer responsibility matters for
both patients.

157
00:29:17,544 --> 00:29:27,696
For the normal station, explain each preparation and perform the
trained simulated support while keeping separate maternal and newborn
observations visible.

158
00:29:27,696 --> 00:29:40,608
For dystocia, state the observed problem, time, help request and
permitted sequence. Demonstrate the difference between suprapubic and
fundal location without applying harmful force.

159
00:29:40,608 --> 00:29:51,504
For hemorrhage, describe mechanism, maternal findings and what changed
after care. Do not claim a numerical blood-loss estimate or successful
treatment without evidence.

160
00:29:51,504 --> 00:30:03,648
Use an original simulated case rather than memorize the school
assessed answer. Real practical signoff still belongs to the
authorized instructor using the actual equipment and criteria.

161
00:30:03,648 --> 00:30:11,424
You should explain the stages and use actual signs to anticipate birth
without an internal exam or a rigid average-time rule.

162
00:30:11,424 --> 00:30:23,376
Rehearse normal support and recognize shoulder dystocia, breech, limb
or cord complications early. Know which school mnemonic actions are
outside the actual paramedic role.

163
00:30:23,376 --> 00:30:34,368
Connect postpartum bleeding to physiology and current permitted care
while maintaining newborn observation. Do not let the emotional
success of birth hide new maternal danger.

164
00:30:34,368 --> 00:30:47,544
Finally, coordinate with midwives and receiving teams and verify both
patients' responses. The next lecture develops newborn ventilation,
compressions and Apgar documentation in detail.
